A||N||12||maninblack||larger throttle body frm fiero store||||5||
Z||000000||maninblack||10-11-2000||09:46 PM||||I recently brought a larger throttle body<BR>frm the fireo store,I wonder if anyone had done this swap before which can lent me some tips.would it be a straight swap or some mods needed to be done?.<BR>thx||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||10-11-2000||10:14 PM||||I had extra throttle body bored out at a local machine shop. It should have been a matter of swapping one for the other, but it wasn't. You might need to trim the opening in the new gasket to fit the bigger bore on your throttle body. The only problem that I had was the opening in the intake was smaller than my new butterfly, so I had to pull the upper manifold and cut the opening to the larger size. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mshill||10-12-2000||01:18 AM||||cooter,  Where did you get the butterfly for the bored TB.  I am thinking of doing this with my V6 setup going into my 3.4L this month.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||10-12-2000||02:00 AM||||I bought the Fiero Store one. You have a few things to do. First the gasket needs to be trimmed for the larger size. (I think they should have supplied one) Then the intake needs to be opened up. (Dremel tool and 2 hours with a fine grade #3 bit) and Finally you have to remove the butterfly and add the angle to the edges. They just tossed in the Butterfly and didn't taper the edge so you have a larger gap and setting the idle is a pain. I couldn't get mine to idle less than 1500 rpm! I just tapered the edges so it was a good seal. The Fiero Store opened up the idle adjust screw so you will need to set the idle at 1000 RPM while the ECM is shorted. (just like when you set the timing)<BR>Then you need to disconnect battery, wait 1 minute, hook it back up and drive the car above 40 mph so the ECM can re-adjust the IAC valve. This was the first product I got from the Fiero Store that I wasn't impressed with the workmanship. Once you get it on the car and set-up, You'll notice a difference above 2000 RPM. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-12-2000||08:41 AM||||The correct procedure for setting the idle is in the haynes book. It tells you how to get the ECM to close the IAC so you can properly adjust the butterfly.||||reg||1
Z||000005||maninblack||10-12-2000||09:10 AM||||thx guys for your info.luckily I asked,it sounds like not a straight swap as they have<BR>said.anyway,its good to know what to be<BR>expect.<BR>thx||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||10-12-2000||01:05 PM||||Darrell Morse bores the TBS and Plenum for $150 including return shipping. The quality is excellent and it includes the gasket matched to the new size. I used his service (TBS boring) for my 3.4 project and I recommend it to every one. Turnaround was around 7 days. It is plug & play except for the mentioned procedure for the IAC readjustment. Total I paid $170. I think that's less than the $199 that FS charges. Also Morse's bore is 1 or 2mm bigger.<BR>You can contact him at:<BR>morse86@aol.com or (612)-441-5679||||reg||14
Z||000007||Cooter||10-12-2000||04:54 PM||||I made my butterfly from some thin, high grade aluminum plate that I had left over from the race karts. I used the stock one as a pattern and drew around it with a sharpie. Then cut away everything that did not look like a butterfly  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>It took some time, but I got it to fit really good. I knife edged the leading side of the butterfly a little to help it fit against the bore of the T/B. Also, it is thinner than stock, so the screws were shortened a little, loc-tited in place and then peened so they may never fall out (hopefully).||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||10-12-2000||05:07 PM||||Dose anyone here know how much the stock throttle body flows in CFM (cubic feet per minute)? I increased the throttle body induction flow 100-CFM on my dyno2000 program and it only shows a 1 horsepower gain and 1 ft. lb. of torque on a stock 3.4-liter engine. I increased it from 500 CFM to 600 CFM. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||tiacPaul||10-14-2000||03:02 AM||||Just this week i wa thinking of buying the larger bore throttle body.  It increases the width from 1.68 in to 2 in.  Any one else use this?  It says 25% power gain an a direct bolt on.  Any feed back PLEASE!  before i dump money into it.||||reg||5
Z||000010||88formula||10-14-2000||09:29 PM||||TiacPaul, there is no way you will gain 25% more power from the larger throttle body. That would be a 35 horsepower gain on a stock 2.8-liter engine. You would be better off with a better exhaust system, headers, or a camshaft and even those modifications alone would not get you 35 horsepower per a modification. Don't waste your money! ||||reg||1
Z||000011||tiacPaul||10-15-2000||09:54 PM||||I have a 2.5.  The chart showed the horsepower going from 80 up to 100.  I thought the 80 seamed rather low,  but the 100 doesn't sound too bad for a 15 min. direct bolt on. . .||||reg||1
Z||000012||rockcrawl||10-15-2000||10:27 PM||||The 2.5's stock 1.68" throttle body flows about 275 cfm. When bored to 2", it flows about 300 cfm. Absolute maximum possible bore is 2.09" due to the casting. You can get throttle plates from Braswell carbueration <A HREF="http://www.braswell.com/," TARGET=_blank>http://www.braswell.com/,</A>  they are only a couple dollars a peice.<BR> I don't think a stock engine would benefit from a larger TB unless other mods are made like cam, manifold porting, ECM tuning, etc..<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
